  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Crafting & Creation Abilities",
    "function": "crafting",
    "elements": ["fire", "arcane"],
    "synergies": ["Works closely with Herbalism and Alchemy \u2013 many cooking recipes use herbs and monster parts that alchemists also covet. It also synergizes with Buff spells: eating a magical meal might stack with or extend the duration of a buff spell. For instance, a \u201cFortifying stew\u201d might extend the length of a cast Barkskin spell on you, or at least not interfere with magical buffs since it\u2019s a separate source."],
    "source": "docx_non_combat_abilities_spells"
  }
}, {
  "id": "doc_artificing",
  "name": "Artificing (Gadgeteering & Constructs)",
  "type": "passive",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A blend of engineering and magic \u2013 building magical gadgets, traps, or mechanical servants. An artificer might create clockwork devices powered by minor enchantments: e.g. a mechanical bird that can deliver messages, a spring-loaded trap that triggers a thunderwave rune, or even a clockwork golem. They can also craft firearms or steampunk-esque devices in settings that allow it. The Eberron D&D setting has the Artificer class which can infuse items with magic temporarily and eventually create a Homunculus Servant or Steel Defender (a small construct that fights or performs tasks). As a concrete example, an artificer could tinker together a trap turret \u2013 a stationary gadget that fires bolts of energy at enemies when they come near. Or craft a flying mechanical drone to scout areas. Function: Largely utility/indirect combat \u2013 providing tools that the party can use. It\u2019s not direct spell-slinging; it\u2019s preparation of helpful devices.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Crafting & Creation Abilities",
    "function": "crafting",
    "elements": ["fire", "ice", "lightning", "arcane"],
    "synergies": ["Artificing goes hand-in-hand with Smithing, Enchanting, and Runes. To make a gadget, you often need parts (smithing provides metal parts), enchantments (to power it), and maybe runes (to program its magical behavior). Also, once built, gadgets can combo with abilities: e.g., set a gadget trap and use Telekinesis to reposition it mid-battle, or throw an invisibility potion on your mechanical scout to let it sneak better. Artificers often support allies by giving them custom gear \u2013 essentially buffing the team\u2019s capabilities through crafted items."],
    "source": "docx_non_combat_abilities_spells"
  }
}, {
  "id": "doc_scribing_and_scrollmaking",
  "name": "Scribing & Scrollmaking",
  "type": "passive",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "The process of writing spells or magic incantations onto scrolls or tomes for later use. A scribe with arcane knowledge can inscribe a spell onto a parchment, creating a spell scroll that anyone with some magical knack can use once. This is essentially a way to \u201cstore\u201d a spell for future casting without needing the caster present. For example, a wizard might spend downtime to scribe a Fireball scroll \u2013 later, a non-wizard ally could read the scroll to unleash that fireball. According to D&D 5e\u2019s crafting rules, a 1st-level spell scroll might take a few days and 100 gp of rare inks to scribe (higher-level scrolls take much longer and more gold). Scribing also includes copying spells into spellbooks (which is how wizards learn new spells; they decipher and transcribe the arcane runes). Another aspect is making rune scrolls or ward papers that, when activated, create some effect (common in eastern fantasy settings). Function: Non-combat creation that allows flexibility \u2013 you effectively create single-use magic items.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Crafting & Creation Abilities",
    "function": "crafting",
    "elements": ["fire", "arcane"],
    "synergies": ["Ties to Librarian or Scholar abilities \u2013 you need to know the spell first or have it written somewhere to copy. It also pairs with Enchanting \u2013 scrolls are like one-use enchanted items. An important synergy is with party resource management: scribes can turn spare time and resources into emergency buttons (scrolls of healing, teleportation, barrier, etc.), which synergize with having a diverse arsenal. It also synergizes with Language/Runic knowledge \u2013 to scribe a spell correctly, you might need to know ancient runes or magical notation, so high Arcana or Linguistics skill is needed."],
    "source": "docx_non_combat_abilities_spells"
  }
}, {
  "id": "doc_transmutation",
  "name": "Transmutation (Material Transformation)",
  "type": "passive",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "Magically changing one material or element into another or altering its properties. A transmuter focuses on spells like Transmute Rock to Mud, Water to Wine, Stone Shape, etc. They can turn a solid wall into mud to create a passage, or vice-versa solidify water into ice for a bridge. In Fullmetal Alchemist (a novel/manga example), practitioners abide by Equivalent Exchange \u2013 \u201cto create something, something of equal value must be lost\u201d \u2013 but they routinely reshape metal, earth, etc. One common transmutation in fantasy literature is turning lead into gold (the alchemical pinnacle). Another is Polymorphing others into different forms (though that borders on a combat effect if used offensively). Non-combat uses include: reinforcing a collapsing cave by transmuting sand to stone, reshaping a broken key to work again, or turning raw materials into finished ones (wood -> wooden ladder).",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Crafting & Creation Abilities",
    "function": "crafting",
    "elements": ["ice", "water", "arcane", "earth"],
    "synergies": ["Transmutation magic synergizes with Crafting skills \u2013 e.g., you can transmute raw iron ore into refined metal ready for blacksmithing, or shape components without tools. It also can set up environmental combo effects: transmute ground to water, then an ally with Lightning zaps it to shock enemies; or transmute air to toxic gas, then Fire ignites it for a non-combat demolition. In puzzle scenarios, a creative party might transmute obstacles away entirely instead of fighting \u2013 truly emphasizing non-combat problem solving."],
    "source": "docx_non_combat_abilities_spells"
  }
}, {
  "id": "doc_mending_and_repair",
  "name": "Mending & Repair",
  "type": "passive",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A handy ability to magically or mechanically repair broken objects. The simplest magical version is the Mending cantrip, which \u201crepairs a single break or tear in an object you touch \u2013 like two halves of a broken key or a torn cloak\u201d. Essentially, it restores an item to working condition as long as you have all the pieces and the break isn\u2019t too large (typically up to a foot long gap). This is invaluable for maintaining gear: shattered sword mend it overnight. Hole in your boat mend the planks. It\u2019s also useful in quests \u2013 e.g., you find an old scroll that\u2019s torn; Mending can make it legible again. Non-magical repair skills (like a tinkerer using tools) fall here too; a person who can fix a wagon wheel on the fly or jury-rig a damaged generator. Function: Doesn\u2019t directly confront enemies, but ensures that equipment and environment are in optimal shape. Clever use: repair a broken bridge to cross a chasm, mend a broken ladder rung quietly while infiltrating, or fix the evil king\u2019s ancient machines to turn them to your side.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Crafting & Creation Abilities",
    "function": "crafting",
    "elements": ["air"],
    "synergies": ["Combines with Destructive abilities in an interesting way \u2013 you can undo certain consequences, giving more freedom to use destructive spells. For instance, an Earth mage might collapse a wall to stop pursuers, then later the party\u2019s mender can fix it if needed. Also helps with Economy/Resource synergy \u2013 mending means you don\u2019t need to carry spares of common items or worry as much about wear and tear. Additionally, a character with both Mending and Dismantling can take things apart and put them back together at will, useful for bypassing traps or rearranging mechanical components in dungeons."],
    "source": "docx_non_combat_abilities_spells"
  }
}, {
  "id": "doc_dismantling",
  "name": "Dismantling (Salvage)",
  "type": "passive",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "The ability to take apart traps, devices, or even magical constructs carefully to either neutralize them or harvest useful components. A rogue with trap-disarming expertise or an artificer unscrewing a machine falls under this. For example, in some RPGs you can \u201csalvage\u201d an item, breaking it down into raw materials. A dismantler might deactivate a complex mechanical trap by removing a gear, effectively disassembling its trigger \u2013 rendering it safe and yielding a gear for later use. Or after defeating a golem, they could salvage its magical core or rare metal plating for crafting. This skill means nothing goes to waste: broken items, enemy siege engines, etc., can be broken down and repurposed. It\u2019s non-combat because it\u2019s about what you do outside of fighting, but it directly reduces threats (disassembling a trap is an alternative to setting it off or avoiding it).",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Crafting & Creation Abilities",
    "function": "crafting",
    "elements": ["ice"],
    "synergies": ["Pairs nicely with Smithing and Artificing \u2013 the materials you salvage (scrap metal, clockwork springs, enchantment crystals) become ingredients for crafting new items. It also complements Stealth/Infiltration \u2013 a dismantler can silently take apart a lock or alarm system rather than brute-forcing it, allowing the party to penetrate defenses without combat. Additionally, it\u2019s a counter to enemy artificers: if the villain has set up automated turrets, a PC with dismantling skills can neutralize those defenses non-violently, leveling the playing field."],
    "source": "docx_non_combat_abilities_spells"
  }
}, {
  "id": "doc_golem_crafting",
  "name": "Golem Crafting (Construct Creation)",
  "type": "passive",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A high-level crafting ability to create animated constructs or automatons that obey your commands. This is the work of master artificers or mages \u2013 for instance, gathering rare materials (clay from a sacred river, a carved stone body, or a metal frame) and performing an elaborate ritual to animate a Golem. Golems (clay, stone, iron, etc.) are traditionally guardians that follow simple instructions. Crafting one might require binding an elemental spirit or using runes of animation. Outside combat, a golem can perform laborious tasks: carrying heavy loads, building structures, digging, etc., tirelessly. In combat, while they can fight (often very strongly), using a golem is somewhat indirect \u2013 you\u2019ve created a pet/companion rather than attacking personally. And they might also serve as defensive shields or distractions rather than kill-machines, depending on how they\u2019re used. Function: This ability is about investment: you spend time and resources to make a long-term servant. It\u2019s akin to enchanting but on a larger scale (creating a semi-sentient ally).",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Crafting & Creation Abilities",
    "function": "crafting",
    "elements": ["ice", "earth"],
    "synergies": ["Golem crafting wraps together Smithing, Enchanting, Runesmithing, and Artificing \u2013 it\u2019s the pinnacle of all those crafts working together to yield a single powerful creation. After creation, it synergizes with Command or Telepathic abilities so you can better direct your golem. Also, if you have Repair/Mending, you can maintain your construct if it takes damage. Even Necromancy has a parallel (raising undead vs. building golems \u2013 some characters might dabble in both as ways to create minions). A crafted golem can also be a platform for further enchantment \u2013 e.g., inscribe additional runes on your iron golem to grant it flame breath, combining multiple disciplines into one formidable ally."],
    "source": "docx_non_combat_abilities_spells"
  }
}, {
  "id": "doc_telekinesis",
  "name": "Telekinesis (Psychokinesis)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "The ability to move objects (or creatures) at a distance with one\u2019s mind or magic, exerting force without physical contact. A telekinetic mage could, for example, lift a fallen boulder pinning an ally, retrieve keys from a hook across the room, or send an enemy\u2019s weapon flying out of their hand. The D&D Telekinesis spell lets the caster concentrate to move a Huge or smaller object up to 30 feet in any direction (if it\u2019s unattended, it moves automatically), or to contest-control a held object or even grapple a creature in the air. Outside combat uses are legion: rearrange furniture with a thought, pull a book from a high shelf, or slowly float a dangerous item in a box without touching it. In combat, telekinesis is often used for battlefield control (e.g., yanking an enemy mage into melee range of your fighters, or slamming doors shut). It\u2019s inherently non-lethal unless you drop something heavy on someone.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Manipulation Abilities (Mental & Physical Manipulation)",
    "function": "manipulation",
    "elements": ["arcane", "air"],
    "synergies": ["Telekinesis synergizes wonderfully with Disarm \u2013 you disarm an enemy, and then telekinetically whisk their weapon away (or even back at them!). It also combos with environmental elements: telekinetically splashing water onto a fire to douse it, or holding an electrified object against a foe. Because it can manipulate objects, it pairs with creative thinking: e.g., use telekinesis to operate a lever from afar while the party prepares an ambush, or to carry a Smoke Bomb around as a moving cover. It\u2019s a very versatile utility power that complements nearly any plan."],
    "source": "docx_non_combat_abilities_spells"
