}, {
  "id": "doc_invisibility_and_camouflage",
  "name": "Invisibility & Camouflage",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "The power to become unseen or blend into surroundings, avoiding detection. Invisibility is a classic spell that literally makes the target invisible (though still physically present). In D&D, a typical invisibility spell lasts for up to an hour, or until the invisible creature attacks or casts another spell. It allows one to slip past guards, escape pursuers, or set up ambushes. Camouflage is a more mundane or minor magical effect where you change colors/texture to match the background (like a chameleon or via an Illusion spell), achieving a similar stealth benefit. These abilities are non-combat in that their goal is to avoid a fight or gain tactical advantage without direct confrontation. For example, a thief could use invisibility to infiltrate a treasure vault without any guards noticing, rather than fighting their way in.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Manipulation Abilities (Mental & Physical Manipulation)",
    "function": "manipulation",
    "elements": ["arcane"],
    "synergies": ["Invisibility pairs extremely well with Silent movement or Silence spells \u2013 being unseen and unheard makes for near-perfect sneaking. It also complements Pickpocketing or Sapping an enemy: an invisible character could steal an important item or knock an enemy out and slip away without combat. Another synergy: using invisibility and then Telepathy to guide your visible allies (\u201cI\u2019ll scout ahead unseen and mentally relay what I find\u201d). In a more magical combo, invisibility plus an illusion decoy can utterly confuse foes: you vanish, an illusory you appears elsewhere, and enemies chase the fake while you\u2019re safely hidden."],
    "source": "docx_non_combat_abilities_spells"
  },
