}, {
  "id": "doc_gravity_manipulation",
  "name": "Gravity Manipulation",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "The power to increase or decrease gravity\u2019s pull in an area or on specific objects. This is more exotic, but some mages or devices can do it. For instance, a gravity mage could make an object extremely heavy (\u201cGravity Well\u201d on an enemy to pin them down) or extremely light (\u201cLevitate\u201d effect, like removing gravity so an object floats) \u2013 note that Levitate in D&D essentially nullifies gravity on one target up to 500 lbs, making it drift upward or stay suspended. Non-combat uses: Lightening the load of a wagon so horses can pull it easier, or letting workers lift a heavy stone by reducing its weight. Or increasing gravity in a local zone to test endurance or keep something dangerous firmly on the ground. In a security sense, a vault might have a gravity trap that slams intruders to the floor. If one can finely control it, gravity manipulation allows simulated telekinesis (make target light, so it floats; make target heavy, so it falls). It can also assist in travel: lower gravity to make huge leaps (essentially personal low-gravity environment).",
