  "id": "doc_memory_alteration",
  "name": "Memory Alteration (Modify Memory)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A delicate and powerful mental ability to change or erase someone\u2019s memories of events. For example, the Modify Memory spell in D&D allows a caster to reshape a target\u2019s recollection of an event up to 10 minutes long, provided the event occurred in the last few hours (or longer at higher levels). The target gets a save if the memory is of something the caster wasn\u2019t part of, but if it fails, the caster can implant a false memory or remove a real one. In practical use: you could make a guard \u201cforget\u201d that you snuck through the gate last night, or even implant a memory that you were officially allowed in. You could heal psychological trauma by removing the memory of it (ethical implications aside), or conversely harm someone by making them forget their best friend (again, dark use). This is clearly non-combat because it\u2019s not about harming someone now \u2013 it\u2019s about information and narrative. It often comes into play after a social encounter: e.g., if you had to charm and lie to get into a restricted area, you might later modify the guard\u2019s memory so they don\u2019t recall you trespassing (covering your tracks).",
