}, {
  "id": "doc_air_control",
  "name": "Air Control (Wind Manipulation)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "The ability to influence air currents and wind pressure. An aeromancer can summon a gust to fill a ship\u2019s sails or disperse poisonous gas from a room. They might create a cushion of air to slow a fall (feather fall effect) or generate an updraft to help allies jump higher or even glide. They can also still the air for stealth (no rustling or howling wind) or create pleasant breezes in stifling conditions. Air is critical for sound propagation too \u2013 controlling air can muffle sound (by disrupting air molecules) or carry whispers farther (like a directed \u201cwind speech\u201d). A skilled air mage could even remove air from a sealed space to suffocate a flame or create a vacuum (though the latter is dangerous to living beings, it could snuff out fires harmlessly).",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Environmental & Elemental Manipulation",
    "function": "elemental",
    "elements": ["fire", "nature", "air"],
    "synergies": ["Air + Fire: Air feeds fire \u2013 a breeze can turn a small flame into a much larger one. In Genshin Impact terms, \u201cAnemo\u201d (air) triggers a Swirl reaction that spreads elemental effects like fire to a wider area. Outside combat, that might mean if you have a sacred incense burning (with some magical property), an air mage can distribute its smoke uniformly through an area (spreading healing aroma or warding scent). Air + Water: can form mist or fog \u2013 e.g., gather moisture and then chill it (with help of cold magic) while dispersing via wind to blanket an area in fog, great for concealment or watering plants gently over a large area. Air + Sound: As mentioned, controlling air pressure and currents can amplify or dampen sound \u2013 a bard could literally \u201cthrow their voice\u201d on the wind or silence an area by stagnating the air (since sound needs a medium). Air + Lightning: thunderstorms! Air control can summon storm clouds (with some water magic aid) and guide lightning strikes by ionizing paths. But also, purely utility: use lightning to electrically charge an area and wind to carry that static \u2013 maybe a stretch, but could be used to magnetize objects remotely (ionized air + metal = slight charge). Air + Poison/Gas: wind can disperse harmful gases or conversely concentrate them in one spot (like blowing all the smoke in a room to one corner and containing it). With Gravity, an air mage could create a temporary low-gravity zone by spinning a vortex (centrifugal force counteracts gravity slightly \u2013 high fantasy physics). Lastly, Air + Flight is redundant but worth noting: air manipulators essentially can grant limited flight \u2013 e.g., \u201cRide the wind\u201d to lift off the ground briefly. Indeed, some systems have air mages fly by default or grant others levitation via updrafts. In sum, air control is versatile for movement, climate control, and subtle manipulation \u2013 it often underpins other effects (e.g., many spells require a breeze or involve air movement implicitly, which an air mage can enhance or suppress)."],
