}, {
  "id": "doc_haste",
  "name": "Haste (Speed Acceleration)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A buff that greatly increases a target\u2019s speed and reaction time for a short period. Under Haste, a person can move and act much faster than normal \u2013 in D&D, the spell doubles the target\u2019s movement speed, improves their reflexes (bonus to AC and advantage on Dexterity saves), and even grants an extra action each turn for things like an extra attack or dash. Non-combat benefits are significant: a hasted individual can run long distances quickly, possibly outrunning vehicles or covering a day\u2019s travel in a few hours. They could complete tasks in a fraction of the time \u2013 useful for emergency repairs or quickly searching an area that\u2019s about to collapse. The buff usually comes with a drawback: after haste ends, the target often experiences a moment of fatigue or \u201ctime lag\u201d (in D&D, they can\u2019t move or act for a round as lethargy hits). Still, Haste is one of the most valued buffs because it multiplicatively enhances productivity and combat prowess.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Buff Abilities (Enhancements & Boons)",
    "function": "buff",
    "elements": ["arcane", "air"],
    "synergies": ["Haste works well with almost any physical pursuit \u2013 e.g., Haste + Crafting: one could forge items at superhuman speed (though caution \u2013 mistakes could happen if going too fast, depending on fantasy physics). Haste + Flight: if a character can fly, being hasted makes them extraordinarily agile in the air. Haste + Multiattack: characters or creatures that can attack multiple times (like a monk with flurry or a multi-limbed entity) benefit even more from haste\u2019s extra action \u2013 but even non-combat, an extra action could mean picking a lock and disarming a trap nearly simultaneously, for example. A fun synergy: Haste + Slow (on enemies) \u2013 you speed up allies and slow down foes, creating a dramatic gap in time perception that can avoid combat (\u201cwe were moving so fast relative to them, they couldn\u2019t really stop us \u2013 we just zoomed through\u201d). Of course, that\u2019s tactical. For utility: Haste + Healing evacuation \u2013 if someone\u2019s badly hurt, haste the one carrying them to the medic. Or Haste + Search and Rescue \u2013 combing a dangerous area (burning building, ticking clock scenario) is much safer/faster with haste. One must manage the fatigue though: maybe combine with a small Stamina buff or Endurance magic to mitigate the crash. In fiction, Haste-like effects appear as \u201cspeed force\u201d or quicksilver time \u2013 allowing heroes to do in seconds what others do in minutes, which often prevents fights from even starting."],
