}, {
  "id": "doc_elemental_resistance",
  "name": "Elemental Resistance (Protect from Energy)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A buff that protects against a specific type of elemental or magical damage \u2013 common types are fire, cold, lightning, acid, or thunder. Protection from Energy (in D&D) gives the target resistance to one chosen damage type, meaning they take only half damage from that source. In-world, that could be like a magical aura that wards off flame (your clothes might smolder but not ignite, your skin is soot-smeared but not burnt). Non-combat, this is hugely useful: walk through a burning building to save someone (fire resist), survive a fall into an icy lake and not die of hypothermia (cold resist), work safely in a lightning-charged environment like around experimental apparatus or during a storm on a ship\u2019s mast (lightning resist), explore a volatile alchemy lab or dragon\u2019s lair with acid puddles (acid resist). It can also protect against environmental extremes (heat of a desert \u2013 fire resistance helps; extreme cold of a mountaintop \u2013 cold resistance helps). Essentially it mitigates natural hazards that would otherwise require lengthy planning or magic to avoid.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Buff Abilities (Enhancements & Boons)",
    "function": "buff",
    "elements": ["fire", "ice", "lightning", "air"],
    "synergies": ["Resistance + Endurance: stack a specific resistance with general endurance and you become very hardy \u2013 e.g., fire resistance + bear\u2019s endurance could let you basically stroll through a lava tunnel (not swim in lava maybe, but walk near it and only sweat a little). Multiple Resistances: If one has a way to layer resistances (say from gear or traits), you can cover many bases \u2013 though typically a single spell covers one type. Some spells like Absorb Elements not only resist but also let you capture the energy to use offensively \u2013 so that\u2019s a synergy of turning defense into offense (absorb a fireball, then next hit you do extra fire damage). Resistance + Healing: you could intentionally expose yourself to something with minimal harm then heal up, essentially tanking environmental damage to accomplish tasks (like slowly wading through acid to open a valve, healing periodically, made feasible by resistance halving each acid burn). Resistance + Trap Springing: a creative use \u2013 if you have, say, lightning resistance, you might volunteer to trigger an electrical trap intentionally and shrug it off, rather than spend time figuring out how to disarm it \u2013 this is avoiding a drawn-out hazard \u201ccombat\u201d with brute defense. Resistance + Allied Abilities: in a friendly-fire situation, you could coordinate where one ally unleashes a big effect and others are protected. For example, if everyone has fire resistance (via buff or fireproof ointment), the wizard can cast a huge flame wave to clear a swarm of pests while the party stands in it safely \u2013 not exactly non-combat if it\u2019s used offensively, but it\u2019s hazard mitigation for the party. On the flip side, if negotiating with a fire elemental or dragon, showing you are literally immune/resistant to its element (via a buff) could be a psychological ploy (\u201cYour flames don\u2019t frighten us\u201d) potentially avoiding conflict. Overall, resistance buffs are about safety \u2013 they let you do things that would normally be deadly, thereby sidestepping natural battles (like man vs wild type battles, or enduring an enemy\u2019s environmental attack forcing them to change tactics).."],
