}, {
  "id": "doc_regeneration",
  "name": "Regeneration (Rapid Healing)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A buff or effect that causes wounds to heal very rapidly over time. For example, the Regenerate spell in D&D gives a creature fast healing (regaining hit points every round) and even regrows lost body parts over minutes. While often thought of in combat (continuous healing each round), out of combat it\u2019s like having superhuman recovery \u2013 one could survive grievous injuries that would normally require weeks of rest in just an hour. Lost a finger in an industrial accident This magic grows it back in 2 minutes. This ability means you can undertake dangerous tasks with less risk: climb that razor-wire fence \u2013 your cuts will seal up moments later; drink tainted water \u2013 your body shrugs it off and repairs itself; endure an ordeal like a ritual scarring or a venom coursing through you, knowing you\u2019ll heal right after. It also massively helps after a conflict: the party can be back at full health (and even limbs restored) without needing expensive potions or long bed-rest, allowing them to press on with their mission (thus avoiding attrition-based encounters).",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Buff Abilities (Enhancements & Boons)",
    "function": "buff",
    "elements": ["water", "nature", "arcane", "air"],
    "synergies": ["Regeneration + Damage Resistance is effectively near-invincibility in short term: take half damage and heal continuously \u2013 it would take a extreme circumstance to bring you down. For non-combat, that combo could let someone, say, walk through a hail of falling rocks in a cave-in to rescue others, because each bruise knits back and each impact is dulled by resistance. Regeneration + Exhaustion removal: often regeneration in fiction not only heals wounds but can invigorate \u2013 you could pair magical regen with a stimulant or a Lesser Restoration spell to remove fatigue, making someone basically unstoppable for a period (like a berserker or a tireless worker to finish a construction overnight). Regenerate + Shapeshifting: in some lore, changing form can heal (werewolves heal when transforming) \u2013 if you had both, you might regrow parts in one form and shift and have them in another. Or a druid with Regeneration buff could wild shape and if that form loses a limb, it might not matter because on reverting they regenerate \u2013 edge cases but interesting. Regeneration + Poison: normally poisons do ongoing damage \u2013 regen can counteract that by out-healing the damage until the body purges the toxin, effectively rendering many poisons moot. Similarly disease. So pairing regeneration with a mild resistance or simply using it proactively means the party can ignore certain environmental threats (\u201cthis swamp disease, we\u2019ll just regenerate through its effects\u201d). Regeneration + Sacrifice: it could enable heroic non-combat moments like \u201cI\u2019ll stay behind and hold the door \u2013 it\u2019s okay if it crushes my arm, I can regenerate later!\u201d or \u201cI\u2019ll feed the vampire some of my blood to appease them \u2013 I\u2019ll recover quickly anyway.\u201d Essentially, regeneration provides a safety net that can turn what would be lethal choices into survivable ones, which can defuse conflicts (sating a monster\u2019s hunger without killing a person, for instance, if that person can regenerate the loss). In everyday utility, it\u2019s also great for long-term missions: no need to carry tons of medical supplies or worry about permanent injuries \u2013 the party faces challenges knowing even a critical injury isn\u2019t the end, allowing them to attempt more daring, non-violent solutions (like leaping from a height to avoid capture, knowing the broken bones will heal on the fly).."],
