}, {
  "id": "doc_healing",
  "name": "Healing (Lay on Hands / Cure Wounds)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "The straightforward magical ability to restore health and cure ailments in others. Healing spells like Cure Wounds knit flesh and close wounds instantly, while abilities like a Paladin\u2019s Lay on Hands can remove poison or disease and heal a set amount of HP by touch. This is arguably the most important non-combat support, because it undoes the effects of violence or accidents. With a competent healer, the group can attempt more diplomatic or risky solutions knowing if it backfires into a scuffle, the healer can quickly patch people up, keeping the situation from escalating (maybe you step between a panicked civilian and a guard and take a stab wound \u2013 healing yourself quickly can show goodwill and prevent further fighting). Healing magic also saves lives on a broader scale: after a battle or disaster, a healer can treat the injured, which can end cycles of vengeance or sorrow that might lead to further conflicts. Lay on Hands, specifically, not only heals but can neutralize a poison \u2013 e.g., if an ally is bitten by a venomous creature during exploration, a paladin can purge that toxin, preventing a deadly encounter with the ticking clock of poison.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Buff Abilities (Enhancements & Boons)",
    "function": "buff",
    "elements": ["fire", "nature", "arcane"],
    "synergies": ["Healing + Buffs: often used in tandem \u2013 e.g., cast Bless or Shield of Faith to prevent some damage, but any that gets through is then healed, an efficient combo to maintain peace of mind. Healing + Emotional Calming: alleviating pain has psychological effects; a calm emotions plus gentle cure wounds could completely pacify a panicking injured person or creature (some creatures fight viciously when wounded, so instant healing might actually cause them to stop perceiving you as a threat or stop their berserker rage). Healing + Poison Resistance: if someone has resistance and still got hurt, healing fixes them up and they continue resisting \u2013 basically stretching how long they can endure something; in negotiation terms, one could endure an ordeal (like a trial by fire or poison drinking contest) with combo of resistance and healing to outlast the opponent, thus winning without a direct fight. Mass Healing (some spells heal multiple targets) combined with Weather control (for example turning a battlefield\u2019s tide by healing your side and washing away blood with rain \u2013 symbolic end of hostilities). In society, Healing + Crafting: a healer supporting craftsmen or laborers can reduce accidents and fatigue, leading to more production \u2013 meaning resources can be allocated to prosperity not war. In summary, healing is the antidote to the damage that could cause conflict \u2013 it keeps allies in good shape, can even bring back someone from the brink (preventing, say, a feud because you saved someone\u2019s loved one). Paladin\u2019s Lay on Hands especially is a versatile healer\u2019s tool \u2013 \u201ccure a disease or neutralize a poison\u201d with a mere 5 points of the pool \u2013 meaning plagues or toxic threats can be halted by a sufficiently strong healer, avoiding humanitarian crises that often lead to conflict. It\u2019s the ultimate non-combat undo button, fostering an environment where injuries and setbacks are not permanent, which in narrative makes characters more confident to choose non-lethal approaches, knowing they can fix mistakes or injuries that result."],
