}, {
  "id": "doc_slow",
  "name": "Slow (Time Dilation Curse)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A spell or effect that dramatically slows down one or more targets\u2019 movements and reactions. Affected creatures move sluggishly, as if time around them has thickened. In D&D, the Slow spell halves the targets\u2019 speed, gives them penalties to defense, and they can barely make one action at a time. Narratively, a slowed person might perceive everything in molasses speed \u2013 their speech slurs, their attempt to swing a sword or run is in ultra slow-mo. As a non-combat tactic, Slow is brilliant for crowd control: e.g., if a mob of people is panicking or rioting, a well-placed Slow can literally chill them out (hard to riot when you can barely walk). Or if a guard spots you, you can slow them and casually walk away while they futilely try to chase. Slow doesn\u2019t harm directly, which makes it a less aggressive option for dealing with foes: you aren\u2019t hurting them, just temporarily neutralizing them. It also could be used to safely handle dangerous creatures \u2013 e.g., slow a hostile animal and back off rather than having to injure it.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Debuff Abilities (Hindrances & Impairments)",
    "function": "debuff",
    "elements": ["dark", "arcane"],
    "synergies": ["Slow + Haste on your allies is a classic mirror \u2013 your side gets faster, the other gets slower, making any conflict extremely lopsided without necessarily spilling blood. Slow + Debuffs: stacking slow with other impairments like Webs or Entangle essentially immobilizes enemies (they\u2019re physically restrained and time-dilated \u2013 virtually stuck in place). Slow + Social Persuasion: a creative use \u2013 if you slow someone who\u2019s agitated, you might talk them down in the meantime (\u201cby the time this wears off, let\u2019s have you convinced not to continue fighting\u201d). Slow + Fleeing: obviously, a slowed adversary can\u2019t chase you, so it\u2019s perfect for retreating or bypassing encounters (e.g., slow the patrolling sentry, then just stroll past while they are like \u201cHey, stoooop\u2026\u201d in drawn-out speech). One caution synergy: Slow + complex tasks \u2013 if an enemy spellcaster is trying to complete a ritual, hitting them with Slow could buy you time to find a non-violent way to stop the ritual (because their casting or drawing of runes is taking forever). Also interesting, Slow + Healing \u2013 if an ally is grievously wounded and about to die in 3 rounds, slowing them might slow their metabolism and buy an extra few rounds to get a heal over (not standard but a clever DM might allow such a physics bending use). In essence, Slow is about impeding progress \u2013 whether that\u2019s an enemy advance, a chase, or the progression of an adverse situation \u2013 giving the protagonists more control and time to resolve things peacefully."],
