}, {
  "id": "doc_curse_of_clumsiness",
  "name": "Curse of Clumsiness (Bane or Hex)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A general jinx that makes a target less effective at everything \u2013 the opposite of a Bless. The Bane spell, for instance, forces targets to subtract a d4 from their attack rolls and saving throws, representing misfortune or lack of confidence. A Hex spell (from warlocks) might impose disadvantage on a certain ability checks for the target (say, everything involving Strength or Dexterity), making them clumsier or less adept. These debuffs in practice might mean the person fumbles more often \u2013 their sword swings veer off-course, their footing slips at the wrong time, etc. In a non-combat context, making someone clumsy or unlucky can defuse a situation humorously or safely: e.g., a cursed bandit trying to show off with their pistol will misfire and perhaps decide it\u2019s not worth continuing; or a guard reaching for an alarm lever might break it accidentally if hexed. It\u2019s a way to sabotage hostile actions indirectly.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Debuff Abilities (Hindrances & Impairments)",
    "function": "debuff",
    "elements": ["fire", "ice", "holy", "dark", "arcane"],
    "synergies": ["Bane + Bless was touched on \u2013 everyone on your side gets better, everyone on their side gets worse, which might convince the other side that fate is not on their side (encouraging surrender without fighting). Hex + Illusion: you could hex an enemy\u2019s Wisdom ability, then attempt an illusion \u2013 their lowered perception makes them more likely to fall for it (in D&D not exactly how hex works, but if they had to roll an Investigation check to disbelieve, disadvantage from hex could help them be fooled). Bane + intimidation: as the enemy keeps failing at small things (because bane subtracts from all their attempted aggressive actions), you can taunt them or persuade them to back down, effectively saying \u201cSee You can\u2019t touch us; better give up.\u201d It\u2019s demoralizing. Curse + Environmental hazards: make someone clumsy and then chase them \u2013 if there are obstacles, they\u2019re more likely to trip or hurt themselves, meaning you don\u2019t have to actually hurt them (maybe leading to a comical self-defeat). Also, some curses could be specific: e.g., \u201cCurse of Binding\u201d might slow a foe each time they move, or \u201cCurse of Leaden Limbs\u201d increases their armor weight \u2013 these stack with actual physical restraints to ensure someone can\u2019t escape custody. We can also think of curses that amplify damage taken (like a vulnerability curse); in a fight, that ends it quicker (though more lethal). But non-combat, a curse of vulnerability could be used to ensure a creature is deterred by something harmless \u2013 e.g., curse a troll with vulnerability to sunlight, now just shining daylight at it (not usually harmful) becomes painful, so it retreats \u2013 you didn\u2019t have to kill the troll, just scare it with a new weakness. Generally, curses tip fate and parameters so the cursed individual has a much harder time succeeding in conflict \u2013 often leading them to flee, surrender, or bungle their aggressive efforts, meaning a potential fight fizzles out or turns in your favor without heavy violence."],
