  "id": "doc_blindness",
  "name": "Blindness (Blinding Curse)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A debuff that takes away a target\u2019s sight, either temporarily or until cured. Blindness/Deafness spell can blind a foe for a while if they fail a save. A blind enemy is severely handicapped \u2013 in combat, their attacks likely miss, and they can\u2019t easily target spells. As a non-lethal way to neutralize someone, blinding is effective: a blinded guard can\u2019t shoot what he can\u2019t see; a blinded monster might panic or lash out wildly while you lead civilians away. It can be used tactically to avoid killing \u2013 e.g., rather than slay the vicious guard beasts, just blind them and slip past. It\u2019s also useful for stopping a fleeing suspect in a city without harming them \u2013 they literally can\u2019t navigate and might stumble into a wall, where you can catch them.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Debuff Abilities (Hindrances & Impairments)",
    "function": "debuff",
    "elements": ["dark", "arcane"],
    "synergies": ["Blindness + Loud Noise: a blinded foe might rely on hearing; if you also create a loud distracting noise (or silence, ironically, to remove audio cues), they\u2019re lost. Blindness + Invisibility: if you blind an opponent, you don\u2019t even need invisibility to hide, but if you have both, you\u2019re doubly safe \u2013 you could stand next to them and they wouldn\u2019t know. Blindness + Debuffs like Slow or Weakness makes a foe pretty much harmless: slow, weak, and blind, they likely won\u2019t land a hit or run anywhere \u2013 you can then talk them down or tie them up. Blindness + Illusions: funny enough, illusions won\u2019t work on a blind foe (they can\u2019t see them), but if you want to prevent casualties, blinding the enemy and then dropping your own scary illusion may cause them to think something happened (they suddenly can\u2019t see and hear an enemy roaring \u2013 maybe they\u2019ll flee thinking a magical beast got them). Blindness + Healing: if you blind someone inadvertently (say a flashbang effect), having healing or a cure ready ensures you can make amends \u2013 that\u2019s more of a safety synergy to keep usage humane. Mass Blindness (via a flash of intense light, for example) can stop a whole group of attackers in their tracks without killing them \u2013 combine that with talking or intimidation to get them to surrender while they\u2019re disoriented. Also, Partial Blindness like smoke bombs or pepper dust in eyes can do similar \u2013 which ties in environmental and skill usage. The magical blindness is nice because it doesn\u2019t cause lasting harm typically (most spells wear off or can be cured). It's a prime example of a debuff that can stop violence without causing lasting injury. Many creatures will retreat or halt when blinded because continuing to fight is perilous for them."],
