}, {
  "id": "doc_sleep",
  "name": "Sleep (Magical Slumber)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "Causing targets to fall asleep suddenly. The Sleep spell can knock out a group of low-HP creatures for about a minute or until they\u2019re woken. This is a classic non-combat finisher: put the guards to sleep, then quietly move past them; or put a rampaging creature to sleep so you can relocate it or escape. Asleep enemies can later be tied up or disarmed so that when they wake, a fight is avoidable (\u201cDon\u2019t bother struggling, you\u2019re surrounded\u201d). Sleep is indiscriminate in some versions (affecting whoever has the lowest HP first), so one must be careful around allies. But it\u2019s generally kind \u2013 it\u2019s not harming, just pacifying.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Debuff Abilities (Hindrances & Impairments)",
    "function": "debuff",
    "elements": ["arcane"],
    "synergies": ["Sleep + Silence: sleeping foes usually can be woken by noise, but if you also silence the area, clanging armor or shouts won\u2019t reach them. This can keep them asleep longer and ensure a quiet getaway. Sleep + Illusions: if someone sees their buddies suddenly collapse, they might suspect magic, but if you also cast an illusion of, say, a gas cloud or a lullaby-playing fairy, you provide an explanation that might deter them from investigating (or cause them to breathe in deeply and also get affected if your sleep hasn\u2019t hit them yet!). Sleep + Boundaries: once asleep, you can draw chalk around them with a magic circle or place barricades so when they wake, they\u2019re contained or at least not immediately a threat. Sleep + Buffs for Allies (like stealth buffs) just ensures you can coup de gr\u00e2ce or tie up sleeping foes without error. Sleep vs. creatures: some beasts or even automata might not be susceptible, but clever use of sleep (like on handlers or riders of a beast) can indirectly deal with a threat. In negotiation scenarios, Sleep on key individuals might allow tempers to cool \u2013 like the classic \u201cmagically put the angry king to sleep for a bit so cooler heads can negotiate, then wake him once a compromise is found.\u201d It\u2019s a bit treasonous, but effective. And obviously, Sleep + Healing \u2013 if someone\u2019s badly hurt and panicking, putting them to sleep while healing them is akin to anesthesia in surgery, a merciful combo. Sleep spell\u2019s limitation is duration \u2013 often a minute or until disturbed \u2013 so it\u2019s a short-term solution, but often that\u2019s enough to avoid an immediate fight or to turn one into a bloodless victory by tying up foes. Out-of-combat, it\u2019s also great for insomnia ironically \u2013 if an NPC hasn\u2019t slept and is making bad choices, a quick enchantment can give them some needed rest. This shows how debuffs can sometimes double as buffs in a narrative sense (sleep spell used benevolently)."],
