  "id": "doc_poison_and_sickness",
  "name": "Poison & Sickness (Non-lethal Toxins)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "Using poisons or magical sicknesses to debilitate rather than kill. For example, a blowdart with a tranquilizer poison that makes a target groggy or knocks them out \u2013 this is a classic non-combat takedown (used often by drow with their sleep poison). Or a Ray of Sickness spell that makes someone physically ill (disadvantage on attacks, possibly vomiting) without directly killing them. Applying a weak paralysis toxin from a plant to an enemy\u2019s drink might cause them to collapse gently rather than leading to a brawl. The advantage of these is subtlety \u2013 often it doesn\u2019t even seem like an attack; the person just falls ill. This can completely avoid a confrontation if, say, you slip a mickey finn to an aggressive drunk at the bar, he passes out, and the fight never occurs.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Debuff Abilities (Hindrances & Impairments)",
    "function": "debuff",
    "elements": ["nature", "arcane"],
    "synergies": ["Poison + Persuasion: an ill guard or enemy might be more pliable \u2013 \u201cyou\u2019re not feeling well, maybe you should sit out this fight\u201d could actually land when they are nauseated from a spell. Poison + Cure: as mentioned, using poison to subdue and then having the antidote to make sure they don\u2019t actually die or to earn goodwill (\u201cI\u2019ve poisoned you \u2013 here\u2019s the antidote; let\u2019s talk.\u201d A bit rough but effective.). Delivery mechanisms: poison works well with darts, arrows (ranged delivery) or traps (set a trap that releases a sleeping gas). This means you can affect targets without direct confrontation. A synergy there: Stealth + Poison \u2013 a rogue can coat a dagger with non-lethal toxin and just nick an opponent; in seconds they collapse with minimal actual harm. Area effect poison (stink clouds) plus wind control to steer it only into the enemy ranks can route them while your side is unaffected, securing a win without lethal force (though inhaled poisons can kill, but let\u2019s assume a subduing agent). Illusory poison: cause someone to psychosomatically feel sick via illusion of spores or something, combined with a minor real irritant \u2013 could scare them off thinking they\u2019re cursed. Poison + Capture again \u2013 once they\u2019re out or weak, capturing is trivial. It\u2019s basically biochemical debuffing. Magical disease might also apply: e.g., a curse that makes someone weak (like \"Slimy Doom\" in D&D causes eventual stun and damage) \u2013 but if you cure them once they\u2019re incapacitated, you\u2019ve disarmed them and then healed them, possibly earning gratitude or at least removing their will to fight. In summary, controlled use of poisons or sickness spells allows one to win or avoid fights by incapacitation, and often can be reversed or wears off, making it a cleaner solution than lethal options."],
