  "id": "doc_deflect_and_redirect",
  "name": "Deflect & Redirect (Projectile Defense)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "The skill or magic to deflect incoming attacks \u2013 like arrows, bolts, or even spells \u2013 and send them off-course or back at the sender. A monk\u2019s Deflect Missiles ability in D&D lets them catch an arrow and even throw it back. Similarly, some spells like Warding Wind can push away projectiles, or Rebuke type abilities reflect damage. Non-combat, this can protect people without necessarily harming the attacker: e.g., bandits fire arrows at villagers, and the hero waves a hand causing all arrows to veer into the ground. The bandits might take that as a sign that continuing is pointless or that the villagers are protected by powerful forces, possibly ending the raid. If using literal reflection (like a magic mirror shield that bounces a spell back), it could deter the caster from further violence (\u201cI\u2019d only be hurting myself\u2026\u201d). Deflection can also save someone from environmental hazards \u2013 say, deflect falling rocks or a collapsing roof to the side to protect those underneath.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Defensive Abilities (Protection & Mitigation)",
    "function": "defense",
    "elements": ["fire", "arcane", "earth", "air"],
    "synergies": ["Deflect + Taunt: one could stand up front and say \u201cCome on, shoot me!\u201d \u2013 then deflect every shot. Combined with a taunt that focuses enemy fire on you, your ability to harmlessly redirect those attacks can demoralize and essentially neutralize the enemy offense (and since you aren\u2019t killing them, once they run out of ammo or spirit, the conflict ends with no deaths). Redirect + Debuff: imagine reflecting a spell back at a caster who is now under the effect of their own slow or blindness \u2013 that\u2019s quite psychologically jarring and might break their will to fight (being hoisted by their own petard). Deflect + Physical Barrier: if you only can deflect a few shots, retreat behind a wall or tree for the rest \u2013 combining methods to avoid damage. Magical Reflection + Negotiation: if a king sees you literally reflect a dragon\u2019s fire breath back at it, he might be convinced to try diplomacy with said dragon since brute force clearly has counters \u2013 your demonstration of defense might encourage non-violent solutions by showing that violence can be tamed. On a smaller scale, parrying and redirecting an opponent\u2019s strike into the ground could disarm them (their weapon breaks or they overextend). That\u2019s deflection combined with skill usage (disarm maneuver). In some settings, adept fighters can even redirect the momentum of enemies (like using Aikido-like moves) \u2013 so synergy with martial arts: you throw an aggressive foe to the ground with their own charge (deflect their body\u2019s attack) which might knock the fight out of them without you doing significant harm. Overall, deflection is an elegant defense that preserves lives on both sides \u2013 no one gets shot, and ideally the shooters realize continuing is futile, paving the way for a ceasefire or surrender."],
