  "id": "doc_stoneskin",
  "name": "Stoneskin (Damage Resistance)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A protective buff that hardens the recipient\u2019s body (skin like stone or bark) to reduce physical harm. The Stoneskin spell in D&D, for example, gives resistance to non-magical bludgeoning, piercing, and slashing damage (half damage from those) for a time. In effect, weapons have trouble cutting or bruising the target. This is defensive \u2013 it doesn\u2019t stop attacks from happening, but it significantly blunts their effect. In a scenario, if bandits strike you and you barely flinch because your skin is hardened, that can sow fear or at least frustration (\u201cOur blades do nothing!\u201d), which may lead them to flee or negotiate. It\u2019s a way to let an enemy tire themselves out on you while you try to talk sense into them or wait for reinforcements, etc. Non-combat wise, a stoneskin person can do risky tasks like walk through thornbushes or debris fields to rescue someone without getting cut up.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
