}, {
  "id": "doc_sanctuary",
  "name": "Sanctuary (Protective Ward)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A holy or magical ward that prevents enemies from directly attacking the warded person unless they resist the effect. In D&D, Sanctuary makes anyone who tries to attack the sanctified target make a Wisdom save; fail and they must choose a new target or waste the attack. It\u2019s basically a \u201cdo no harm\u201d aura \u2013 you can\u2019t bring yourself to hit this peaceful-looking individual. This is the ultimate defensive stance for pacifists: cast Sanctuary on yourself (or the diplomat, or the medic) and you can walk through a battlefield largely untouched \u2013 if someone attempts to strike, the magic stays their hand (unless they\u2019re very strong-willed). It immediately de-escalates one-sided aggression: for example, a brash youth tries to stab the wise old priest, but Sanctuary causes the youth\u2019s strike to falter, then perhaps guilt takes over as the priest does not retaliate \u2013 a narrative outcome, but plausible. Mechanically, it forces enemies to either pick a different target (hopefully one that can handle it or also has sanctuary) or do nothing. It ends if the sanctuaried person attacks, which enforces a non-violent behavior \u2013 perfect for, say, humanitarian workers in a warzone or a negotiator walking into an angry mob.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Defensive Abilities (Protection & Mitigation)",
    "function": "defense",
    "elements": ["holy"],
    "synergies": ["Sanctuary + Mass Suggestion: you protect yourself with sanctuary so you\u2019re not harmed while you propose a peaceful solution via suggestion magic to all around \u2013 they physically can\u2019t bring themselves to hit you immediately, giving your suggestion a chance to take hold. Sanctuary on multiple party members: if the whole party refrains from attacking and each has Sanctuary (or is in an aura of peace), foes literally cannot initiate hostilities effectively \u2013 they\u2019d fail saves and be forced to stand down or attack someone else (maybe causing intra-conflict confusion). That can defuse a fight at its onset, ideally buying time to resolve the tension. Sanctuary + Healing: classic use \u2013 a cleric with sanctuary can move around a skirmish healing allies, and enemies struggle to stop them because of the ward. This prolongs the allies' endurance and often can turn battle tempo enough that the enemy gives up (if they see their efforts are continually negated by healing and they can\u2019t even hit the healer). Sanctuary + Shield: for a true pacifist who might still get AOE or stray arrow damage, stacking Sanctuary with a high AC or a physical shield means even if someone overcomes the sanctuary effect by force of will, they might still miss due to the shield \u2013 a double layer of safety without offense. Sanctuary + Intimidation: possibly if enemies repeatedly fail to muster the will to attack you, they might think you\u2019re an invincible saint or something, intimidating them spiritually \u2013 especially if combined with a display of divine favor (like glowing eyes or an angelic chorus sound effect from Thaumaturgy). Lastly, Sanctuary on the VIP during an escort \u2013 your group can fight defensively while the protected NPC cannot be targeted, ensuring their survival without needing the NPC to fight back. It\u2019s a purely defensive, non-violent stance, representing holy innocence or diplomatic immunity in magical form, extremely useful to prevent violence to the warded person and by extension to calm situations."],
