  "id": "doc_antimagic_field",
  "name": "Antimagic Field (Magic Nullification)",
  "type": "active",
  "class": "universal",
  "description": "A powerful defensive sphere that suppresses all magic within it. In an antimagic field, spells don\u2019t function, summoned creatures wink out, and magical attacks or buffs are inert. This is defensive in the sense that it protects against magical harm absolutely (while also canceling your own magical advantages). It\u2019s the \u201cguns off\u201d switch for a wizard duel, making it so conflict must either halt or proceed with mundane means. If you have antimagic and the opponent is heavily reliant on magic, they likely can\u2019t or won\u2019t fight you, leading to a standoff or negotiation. It can also protect against magical disasters \u2013 e.g., an active portal spewing demons can be shut down temporarily by antimagic, stopping a chaotic battle while you arrange a more permanent solution.",
  "level": 1,
  "template": {
    "category": "Defensive Abilities (Protection & Mitigation)",
    "function": "defense",
    "elements": ["arcane"],
    "synergies": ["Antimagic + Physical Defense: since your magical defenses drop inside (no magical shields, no buffs), you\u2019d pair antimagic with strong mundane armor or cover, making you still safe from any physical attacks the enemy resorts to. Antimagic + Law Enforcement: this combo is more narrative \u2013 use antimagic field to enable arresting a rogue mage without incident (they can\u2019t cast to resist, so it stays peaceful). Antimagic + Parley: set up an antimagic zone for peace talks so neither side can use magic tricks or attacks \u2013 that assurance might bring both parties to ease (like a neutral ground). Antimagic + Creature containment: if a rampaging enchanted creature (like a golem or possessed person) is causing havoc, dragging them or luring them into an antimagic zone could disable them safely (possession ends, golem stops), ending the conflict without destroying the creature/host. Antimagic + Technology: in settings where tech exists, flipping off magic can allow technological or physical solutions to shine \u2013 basically leveling the field if your side is weaker in magic but stronger in another aspect, forcing conflict into that domain or convincing foes who depended on spells to reconsider fight entirely. It\u2019s a heavy, high-end ability often, but in its presence, magical violence becomes impossible, forcing combatants to rely on wit or withdraw. That in itself can end fights early \u2013 e.g., an aggressive sorcerer seeing their fireballs fizzle might flee rather than face you with a dagger. Conversely, a gentle healer could use antimagic defensively to stop an evil warlock\u2019s curses from hitting innocents while evacuating them. It\u2019s the ultimate \u201cstop all this magic nonsense\u201d defensive measure, which often pressures adversaries toward non-magic (and likely non-violent) alternatives when used strategically."],
